events.thought:
Now I'll write the post-receive hook. I'll use `write` for the hook file. The hook runs as the git user, and needs to write to /var/www/main and /var/www/dev (already chown'd to git).

Hook:
```bash
#!/bin/bash
# Post-receive deploy hook: deploy main -> /var/www/main, dev -> /var/www/dev
GIT_DIR=/git/project
export GIT_DIR

while read oldrev newrev refname; do
    branch="${refname#refs/heads/}"
    [ "$branch" = "$refname" ] && continue  # not a branch
    case "$branch" in
        main) dest=/var/www/main ;;
        dev)  dest=/var/www/dev ;;
        *)    continue ;;
    esac
    if [ "$newrev" = "0000000000000000000000000000000000000000" ]; then
        # branch deleted: clear the deploy directory
        rm -rf "$dest"
        mkdir -p "$dest"
        continue
    fi
    mkdir -p "$dest"
    # delete old files (preserve the directory), then extract new tree
    find "$dest" -mindepth 1 -delete
    git archive "$newrev" | tar -x -C "$dest"
    echo "Deployed $branch ($newrev) to $dest"
done
```

Note: the hook runs with cwd = git dir, and GIT_DIR='.' is set. I'll explicitly set GIT_DIR=/git/project to make it absolute — safer.

The `git archive $newrev` using the commit's rev avoids HEAD-related issues.

Next, I'll write the nginx config. I'll create /etc/nginx/sites-available/gitproject and make a symlink in sites-enabled.

```nginx
server {
    listen 8443 ssl;
    listen [::]:8443 ssl;
    server_name localhost;

    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/self-signed-localhost.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/self-signed-localhost.key;

    root /var/www/main;
    index index.html;

    location /dev/ {
        alias /var/www/dev/;
        index index.html;
    }
}
```

Wait: when `root /var/www/main` is set on the server, `location /dev/ { alias /var/www/dev/; }` — for the request /dev/index.html, alias replaces the matched prefix /dev/ with /var/www/dev/, giving the path /var/www/dev/index.html. Good.

Should I also handle the case of a request to exactly `/dev`? Not needed for the test (it's /dev/index.html). No problem.

